# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore — what `COPY` is not allowed to see
# =============================================================================
#
# THIS IS NOT .gitignore, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS
# --------------------------------------------------
# .gitignore keeps files out of version control.
# .dockerignore keeps files out of the BUILD CONTEXT — the tarball your machine
# uploads to the Docker daemon before the first instruction even runs.
#
# Without this file, `docker build .` on this project would ship .git, .venv,
# node_modules, the local database and every scan JSON to the daemon. That is
# hundreds of megabytes copied on EVERY build, before anything is cached.
#
# Three reasons it matters, in increasing order of importance:
#
#   1. SPEED     — the context is uploaded in full every single build.
#   2. CACHING   — `COPY . .` hashes the context. If .venv is included, a
#                  stray .pyc changes the hash and busts every later layer.
#   3. SECRETS   — anything copied in is inside the image FOREVER, in a layer.
#                  Deleting it in a later RUN does not remove it; the layer is
#                  still there and `docker history` will show it. A .env copied
#                  in by accident is a published credential.
#
# Rule of thumb: deny broadly, then allow back what you actually need.
# =============================================================================

# --- Version control ---------------------------------------------------------
# .git can be larger than the whole application, and carries every version of
# every file ever committed — including anything you later removed.
.git
.gitignore
.github

# --- Python artefacts --------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# --- Virtual environments ----------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ Critically important. A .venv holds platform-specific compiled binaries
# built for YOUR machine. Copied into a Linux image they are at best dead
# weight and at worst shadow the correctly-installed packages, producing
# import errors that make no sense.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# --- Local state and real data ----------------------------------------------
# The database and scan outputs are RUNTIME state. Baking them into the image
# means every deployment resets to a snapshot of your laptop, and anyone who
# pulls the image gets your scan history. State belongs in a volume.
data/
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# --- Secrets -----------------------------------------------------------------
# .env holds real values. .env.example is placeholders and IS committed — but
# neither is needed in the image, because config comes from the environment at
# run time, not from a file baked in at build time.
.env
.env.*

# --- Documentation and tests -------------------------------------------------
# Excluded to keep the image small and the attack surface minimal. Tests run in
# CI against the source tree, not inside the shipped image.
#
# (There is a school of thought that says ship the tests so you can run them
# against the real artifact. Reasonable, and if you adopt it, delete the
# `tests/` line rather than working around it.)
docs/
tests/
*.md
!README.md

# --- Editor and OS noise -----------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# --- Node (the frontend has no build step today, but this is cheap insurance) -
node_modules/
npm-debug.log

# --- Docker's own files ------------------------------------------------------
# The Dockerfile does not need to be inside the image it builds.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
